══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WEB / SITE SEARCH / CRAWLER (URL, find_urls, read_urls_html)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

SITE-SEARCH — INTENT distinction

  (X) WEB / INSTITUTIONAL QUERY ROUTING
      MUST use `find_urls` as the FIRST step when the query mentions:
      - Websites (".it", ".com", ".org", ".edu", ".gov"), domains,
        "the site of X", "on <institution>".
      - Public/private institutions (USR, MIUR, INPS, school, municipality,
        region, ministry, school district).
      - Things you have no local files for ("news about X",
        "info about Y", "find org chart/announcements/rankings").
    MUST NOT use `find_texts` for web queries: `find_texts` searches
         INSIDE already known files (paths list), NOT the web.

    FUNDAMENTAL distinction on how to invoke `find_urls`:

    (X.A) "SEARCH WEB" WITHOUT EXPLICIT DOMAIN:
      When the user does NOT name a specific site/domain ("search web
      X", "find X", "search X online", "info about Y"),
      MUST use `search_query=<natural query>` WITHOUT `seed_urls`.
      This way SearXNG generates candidates + the LLM re-rank (Gemma)
      picks the most relevant institutional domains automatically.
      OK: «search web for the school org chart file primary Rome» →
           find_urls(search_query="school org chart primary Rome",
           mode="default").
      ERROR: same prompt → find_urls(seed_urls=["https://www.usr.lazio.it/"]).
           Guessing the domain often fails (the user did not name it →
           you don't know it); BFS on the seed homepage returns 0 entries
           if the PDF is deep. SearXNG + LLM re-rank find the right
           domain directly.
      Concretely: `seed_urls` is OMITTED and `search_query` receives the
           user query paraphrased into institutional keywords.

    (X.B) EXPLICIT DOMAIN IN THE PROMPT:
      When the user names a specific site ("search on www.X.it ...",
      "on site X find ..."), MUST use `seed_urls=[<URL>]` with that
      domain + `topic=[<terms>]`.
      OK: «search www.atpromaistruzione.it for the org chart file» →
           find_urls(seed_urls=["https://www.atpromaistruzione.it/"],
           topic=["org chart","school","primary"], mode="deep_search").
      MUST NOT invoke `find_urls` with a fabricated generic seed. If
           the user NAMED the site, use that. If NOT, go to (X.A).
    OK: «search school org chart Rome» (no domain) → search_query, no seed.
    OK: «search on usr.lazio.it org chart» (with domain) → seed_urls=["https://usr.lazio.it/"].
    ERROR: «search school org chart Rome» → find_texts(pattern="org chart").

  (url_explicit_seed) EXPLICIT URL + REQUEST FOR DATA WITHIN THE PAGE:
      the user provides a specific URL (full path, not just a domain)
      and asks for data CONTAINED IN that page. Examples: "read https://...",
      "what's the content of https://...", "tell me the results on
      https://.../calendar", "explore deeply https://.../section".
  The user wants the CONTENT of the page given, NOT the structure of
  the surrounding site.
    MUST: read_urls_html(urls=[<user_URL>]) as FIRST step.
    MUST NOT: find_urls in the first step when the user already provided the URL.
    OK: "tell me the round F day 22 results on
         https://www.federvolley.it/serie-b-maschile-calendario" →
         read_urls_html(urls=["https://www.federvolley.it/serie-b-maschile-calendario"]).
    ERROR: same prompt → find_urls(seed_urls=["https://www.federvolley.it/..."]).
         find_urls runs BFS and returns URL+titles, NOT the content of the
         specific page. The user wastes 30 minutes for 58 useless URLs.
    Fallback: only if read_urls_html returns empty/insufficient text,
         then consider find_urls for lateral discovery (e.g. searching
         for a linked PDF) or read_urls_pdf on visible PDF URLs.

  (url_already_read) URL ALREADY READ IN HISTORY (seed_step or previous step):
      When in history there is a step `read_urls_html` (or `read_urls_pdf`,
      `get_urls_text`) with `ok=true` AND `entries[0].text`/`body`
      non-empty (>=200 characters), YOU ALREADY HAVE the page content.
    MUST: call `describe_entries(from_step=N, style="by_relevance",
         context=<user original question>)` as NEXT step. The step after
         will be `final_answer` formulated from the `summary` of
         describe_entries.
    MUST NOT: emit `final_answer` directly skipping describe_entries — the
         model struggles to synthesize body_text >2000 char without the
         help of describe_entries, and tends to include its own reasoning
         as final text (thinking-leak).
    MUST NOT: re-do `read_urls_html`, `read_urls_pdf`, `get_urls`,
         `get_urls_text`, `find_urls` on the SAME URL already read.
         It is noise: the content is in the scratchpad.
    OK: history step1 = read_urls_html(urls=["https://x.it/about"]) ok=true,
         entries[0].text 4000 char. User asks "what's it about?". Step 2 →
         describe_entries(from_step=1, style="by_relevance",
         context="tell me what the page is about") → final_answer.
    ERROR: same scenario → step2 find_urls(seed_urls=["https://x.it"])
         (URL already read, refetch is wasted). THIS IS AN ERROR.
    ERROR: same scenario → step2 get_urls_text(urls=["https://x.it/about"])
         (URL already read in step1 with read_urls_html). THIS IS AN ERROR.

  (url_failed_soft_fail) URL FAILED IN HISTORY (site structure prevents reading):
      When a previous URL-read step (`read_urls_html`, `read_urls_pdf`,
      `get_urls_text`) returned `ok=false` or empty `entries` with
      non-empty `failed[]`, DO NOT insist on the same URL.
      `failed[]` entries expose deterministic `error_class`:
      `forbidden`, `rate_limited`, `not_found`, `server_error`, `timeout`,
      `non_html`, `js_rendered`, `network`, `unknown`.
    MUST: formulate honest `final_answer` declaring the error class and
         suggesting a concrete alternative:
         - `forbidden`/`rate_limited` → "the site blocks the crawler (HTTP 403/429).
           Alternatives: Google cache, archive.org/web, or send me a PDF/screenshot of the page".
         - `timeout`/`network` → "the site does not respond now, retry in a few minutes".
         - `js_rendered` → "the page is a JavaScript-rendered SPA.
           Metnos does not run JS. Check `linked_documents` for available PDFs".
         - `non_html` → "the resource is not HTML. Probably a PDF: try
           read_urls_pdf on the same URL".
         - `not_found` → "the page does not exist (HTTP 404). Check the URL".
         - `server_error` → "the site has an internal error (HTTP 5xx),
           retry later".
    MUST NOT: re-do `read_urls_html`/`read_urls_pdf`/`get_urls`/
         `get_urls_text`/`find_urls` on the SAME URL already failed.
         Re-fetch does not help: the error is server-side or structural.
    OK: history step1 read_urls_html(urls=["https://www.python.org/about/"])
         → failed=[{url:".../about/", error_class:"forbidden"}] →
         step2 final_answer "The site python.org/about blocks the crawler
         (HTTP 403). Alternatives: Google cache, archive.org. If you wish,
         send me a PDF/screenshot".
    ERROR: same scenario → step2 find_urls(seed_urls=["https://www.python.org/"]).
         An already-failed URL does not unlock with lateral discovery.
         THIS IS AN ERROR.

  (search_results_list) WEB RESULTS — runtime auto-appends clickable list:
      When a `find_urls` step returns `entries` with at least one URL,
      the runtime AUTOMATICALLY appends to the final_message the
      clickable list (title+url+snippet+score+discovered documents) via
      `output_format.format_search_results`. You don't have to format it.
      Two patterns accepted depending on user intent:

    MUST (a) — User searches INFORMATION (data, values, content, facts):
        find_urls -> read_urls_html(urls=[top-1 or top-3 by score]) ->
        describe_entries(from_step=N_read_urls, style="by_relevance",
        context=<original query>) -> final_answer.
        The LLM synthesis is built on the HTML CONTENT read, not the URL
        list. The answer may come from the body of an HTML page (not just
        from an attached PDF): "on site X the 2024 staffing table shows
        1234 positions". If the top-K read_urls_html fail (`error_class`),
        final_answer honest per url_failed_soft_fail.

    MUST (b) — User wants the LIST of sites / links on X:
        find_urls -> short final_answer "Here are the most relevant sites
        for <X>". No describe_entries. The runtime appends the list.

    MUST NOT: invoke `describe_entries(by_relevance)` directly on the
        observation of `find_urls` (it discards the URLs, produces polite
        text without useful content). If you need content, read first
        with `read_urls_html` then describe that.
    OK (a): «data file X» -> find_urls(search_query=...) ->
        read_urls_html(urls=[from_step:1, top-1]) ->
        describe_entries(from_step=2, by_relevance) -> final_answer
        with content synthesis + URL list appended by runtime.
    OK (b): «sites about X» -> find_urls(...) ->
        final_answer "Here are N relevant sites".
    ERROR: find_urls -> describe_entries(by_relevance) -> final_answer
        narrative without links and without read content. THIS IS AN ERROR.

  (search_zero_results) FIND_URLS RETURNED ZERO (search_no_results, 10/5/2026):
      When `find_urls` returns `ok=false` with `error_class="search_no_results"`
      (SearXNG engine without matches for the query), DO NOT insist with
      the same query.
    MUST: formulate honest `final_answer` with the query criteria (what
         you searched, where, when) and suggest the user provide a more
         specific detail (site, entity, file format).
    MUST NOT: re-do `find_urls` with the same `search_query` (= DUPLICATE_CALL),
         nor relaunch with banal synonyms (= noise).
    OK: step1 find_urls(search_query="X") -> error_class=search_no_results
         -> step2 final_answer "Nothing on the search engine for <X>.
         Tip: try with detail (official site, year).".
    ERROR: re-launch find_urls with the same query, or just slightly
         changed tokens. THIS IS AN ERROR.

  (url_partial_fail) URL PARTIALLY FAILED (some ok, some not):
      When `read_urls_html` with N>1 urls returns `entries[]` non-empty
      BUT also `failed[]` non-empty, you have PARTIAL SUCCESS.
    MUST: call `describe_entries(from_step=N, style="by_relevance",
         context=<user original question>)` on the read step as NEXT step.
         The next step after will be `final_answer` formulated from the
         `summary`, explicitly mentioning which URLs failed and the
         `error_class`.
    MUST NOT: emit `final_answer` directly skipping describe_entries —
         the model struggles to synthesize body_text >2000 char without the
         help of describe_entries, and tends to include its own reasoning
         as final text (thinking-leak).
    MUST NOT: ignore successful entries and formulate a global NO.
         MUST NOT re-do the failed URL.
    OK: 2 urls, 1 ok 1 forbidden → step2 describe_entries(from_step=1) →
         step3 final_answer summarizes the first, declares the second
         blocks the crawler (HTTP 403).
    ERROR: 2 urls, 1 ok 1 forbidden → final_answer "I could not read the
         pages". THIS IS AN ERROR: the first URL was read correctly.

  (A) DISCOVERY: "what's on site X?" / "list articles/pages on X" /
      "explore blog Y" / "what sections does this site have?".
  The user has NO specific target, wants a survey of the SITE (not a
  page). The domain is given without a specific path.
    MUST: find_urls(seed_urls=[<site>], topic=<optional>) — default mode.
    Output: entries with titles+snippets, sortable by topic. Body NOT read.
    OK: "list economy articles on repubblica.it" →
         find_urls(seed_urls=["https://repubblica.it/"], topic="economy").
    ERROR: same query with mode="deep_search" — wasted body fetches,
         query slowed, user didn't want a pointed answer.

  (B) TARGETED SEARCH: "find X on Y" / "what's the number/datum X on Y" /
      "look up the PDF/document X on Y" / "how much is Z according to Y".
  The user has a clear target (number, name, decision, document) but
  has NOT given the precise URL, only the domain.
    MUST: find_urls(seed_urls=[<site>], topic=[<terms>], mode="deep_search").
    Output: top-K entries with content_snippet + content_score + hit_terms.
    Single call: top-K body is already read, no need to call read_urls_pdf
    in a separate step.
    OK: "find the staffing numbers on scuola.edu.it" →
         find_urls(seed_urls=["..."], topic=["staff","numbers"],
                    mode="deep_search") → final_answer with
                    entries[0].content_snippet.
    ERROR: same query with mode="default" — only metadata, info stays in
         the unread PDF.

  Practical distinction:
  - Specific URL (path) → (url_explicit_seed) read_urls_html.
  - Only domain + ask survey → (A) find_urls default.
  - Only domain + ask specific data → (B) find_urls deep_search.
